 Goal: the RTOS and Linux both running on `nikon-z63`, with a display, working
 menus, the ISP, and the rest of the camera actually doing something.
 
-Where this starts, measured rather than assumed:
-
-* **Linux already works** — boots on CPU3 to an interactive BusyBox shell with
-  a serial console. `z63-tools/smoke.sh` is the regression.
-* **The RTOS boots and then stops.** All three cores reach the TOPPERS idle
-  task. The application layer runs only between 3.5% and 4.1% of a 60-second
-  run and never again.
-* **Why it stops is known exactly.** The last application code executed is
-  `0x402B0368`, inside `sub_402B0300`, the display subsystem's init: it
-  configures both DISP units from the `.data` descriptors at 0xA02808440 and
-  0xA02808478, writes 0x1548F000 / 0x15491000 / 0x15492000, registers five
-  interrupt handlers through `sub_40C00704`, then calls `sub_401854AC` and
-  never returns. That function is a TOPPERS **blocking** service call — it
-  does the E_CTX context check and the E_ID range check against the count at
-  0xA02BD2318, over 40-byte control blocks at 0xA04D27D90. It is waiting for
-  an event that nothing will ever signal.
-
-**So the one thing standing between here and everything else is interrupts.**
-No device model in this board raises an IRQ. Three independent reversing
-passes hit the same wall from three directions: the storage model traced no
-name-to-SPI binding and wired nothing; the HDMI driver's status dispatcher
-`0x402B6BE8` has no pointer to it anywhere in text, rodata or `.data`, so it
-is installed from a table built at run time; and the USB assist arm at
-`sub_40C716E4` spins `do { delay(10); } while (flag != 1)` with no bound and
-no register in the loop, the flag set only by an ISR. Modelling more registers
-changes nothing until something can pull a line.
-
-Running notes on what is known live in `docs/symbols.md` — every function
-identified so far, with how it is known. Add to it as each phase names more.
-
-## Phase 0 — integrate, and build the loop that drives every later phase
-
-1. Wire `nikon-z63-hdmi.h` and `nikon-z63-usb.h` into the board. Resolve the
-   overlap first: `nikon-z63-display.h` maps 0x15496000+0x3000 as refuse-only
-   and the HDMI model owns that span.
-2. `z63-tools/smoke.sh` stays green: RTOS to idle with no unmodelled access,
-   Linux to BusyBox.
-3. Write `z63-tools/where_blocked.py`. It automates by script what was done by
-   hand to find the answer above: run with `-d exec`, find the last execution
-   in the application regions, print the hand-off, and name the containing
-   functions. **Every later phase is a turn of this crank**, so it has to be
-   one command.
-
-Exit: both models in, smoke green, and one command that answers "where is it
-stuck now".
-
-## Phase 1 — interrupts
-
-1. Settle how a handler reaches a line. `sub_40C00704(id, 0, handler)` is the
-   registration the display init uses with ids 99, 100, 101, 102 and 105;
-   establish whether that first argument is an SPI or an index into a table,
-   and find what consumes it. The IRQ name table at rodata 0x41847000 gives
-   SPI *n* at `0x41847000 + 0x20n` — that arithmetic already reproduces the
-   board's `Z63_UART_IRQ_SPI 173` from `UART_0_RX`, so it is trustworthy.
-2. Give the board the GIC lines for the devices already modelled, from that
-   table: `DISP_0/1/1B/DWCH` 67-70, `MIPI_DSI_TX` 73, `HDMI_MASTER/CEC` 71-72,
-   `EXS_SDIO_*` 97-99, `EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH30/40_*` 100-104, USB 108-110.
-3. Make the display model raise its line when the firmware arms it, and only
-   then. The rule the whole project runs on applies here too: an interrupt the
-   firmware did not ask for is an invented device behaviour.
-
-Exit: the RTOS gets past `sub_402B0300` and new application code executes.
-
-## Phase 2 — turn the crank
-
-> **Status, loader round.** `z63-tools/smoke.sh` **PASSES both checks**: the
-> RTOS runs 45 s with nothing stopping, and Linux reaches a BusyBox prompt in
-> the same machine (146 serial lines, 40,917 distinct blocks). The RTOS's own
-> serial console now works, which replaces the block count as the instrument
-> of record. Three things got it there, in order of size:
->
-> 1. **The image placed in DRAM where the firmware's initial page tables
->    expect it** -- the loader's job, see `serial-nor-resources.md`.
->    Undefined-instruction exceptions 102,715 per 30 s -> **0**.
-> 2. The loader band itself, `-machine loader=<file>`, which removed the
->    stop at pc 0x40168a04.
-> 3. Six more blocks modelled, listed in `register-windows.md`.
->
-> **Two open questions this round opened, both worth more than another
-> block:**
->
-> * **How the body really starts Linux.** The firmware now gets far enough
->   to release CPU3 itself -- and it asks for it at **0x40160000, the RTOS
->   image entry**. It is claiming CPU3 as a fourth RTOS core, not handing it
->   to Linux. So `-machine cpu3-entry` is a stand-in that fires early, and
->   the board now says so loudly every run while keeping it, because it is
->   the only thing that boots Linux today. Finding the real trigger is the
->   next structural piece: it is probably the IPC bridge, and
->   `nikon-ipc-bridge` has both ends of that path already.
-> * **The IPC hand-off was ordered wrong, and that is fixed.** LaMainApp
->   attaches to the share_mem directory the RTOS builds -- `sub_405B08`
->   does `memcmp(page + 8, "MASTER", 7)` and returns NULL on mismatch, at
->   which point `CIpcPorting_Initialize` prints `...[105], error.` and the
->   IPC layer is dead for that run. Linux reaches userspace in ~3 s; the
->   emulated RTOS needs far longer; the stand-in started Linux at machine
->   reset, so Linux always lost a race the body never has. Proved by
->   killing LaMainApp and re-running it once the RTOS was up: the error
->   disappears. `-machine li-wait-ipc` (on by default) now holds CPU3 until
->   that header appears. **This is not the display's gate** -- see below --
->   but it was wrong on its own terms.
-> * **The display still has no frame.** The scanout refuses with `output
->   size is 0x0 (mode 0, from +0x132c/+0x1328)` -- unchanged. The crank has
->   not yet reached whatever writes it. That is Phase 3 and it is now the
->   top of the list.
->
->   **Menu drawing is the RTOS's job, not LaMainApp's** (corrected by the
->   project owner). So the display path does not run through the Linux
->   application at all, and the IPC fix above, while real, does not open
->   it. What is known: `sub_402B20EC` writes the output size at +0x132c /
->   +0x1328 and **never runs**; its call chain tops out after four
->   functions in handlers that are only reached indirectly; the display
->   subsystem's own event loop (`sub_402BCEB8`, a three-way dispatch) *is*
->   alive and has taken all three of its branches; and the subsystem was
->   still entering blocks it had never entered before at 99.7% of the way
->   through a 60 s run. So it is progressing, not deadlocked -- it has not
->   yet been given the event that sets a mode. The open question is which
->   RTOS task sends it and what that task is waiting for.
-
-> **Status, NAND round.** The "idle, not deadlocked" reading in
-> `register-windows.md` predates the loader round and no longer holds. Measured
-> since, in order of what each step uncovered:
->
-> 1. **CPU0 was never idle.** It sat at 0x40c5e530 in task STSQNAND (priority
->    4), polling 0x17800014 for a COMP bit plain storage never sets, retrying
->    for ever; the 87 tasks on that core -- APPCTL, SYSCTL, GUISRV, APPMENU,
->    APPLV, TFTDIR among them -- were runnable and starved. `z63-tools/tasks.py`
->    now names every task (exinf points at SOLID's task object; the name is at
->    +0x1d) and shows state, priority, core and entry.
-> 2. **EXS1780 is a Cadence NAND controller** (SPI 105, `EXS_NF`) holding the
->    firmware's "ANAND" store. `nikon-z63-nand.h` models it with an erased
->    1 Gbit chip, or `-machine nand=<file>` to keep what the firmware writes.
->    The store mounts, finds nothing, formats.
-> 3. **That let the module lifecycle reach group 4 -- component step 4**, the
->    value the display gate `sub_4074B484` waits for. The round driver
->    `sub_40D6FB64` runs on three tasks (PKGB1/2/3, one per core), meeting at
->    an event-flag barrier after each of seven groups; the waits after groups
->    0-4 time out at 2000 ticks. Group 5 waits for all four storage
->    sequencers; group 6 parks the two other cores in WorkSRAM and suspends
->    DRAM -- the end of a boot, not a crash.
-> 4. **Without the per-unit SNOR_USER data, that lifecycle desynchronises.**
->    Component `sub_407640E0`, step 3, builds a mask with
->    `sub_40321450({n, ...})`, which loops `n - 1` times; `n` comes from
->    configuration object 28 and reads 0, so it loops 2^32 times. PKGB3 spends
->    ~10 s there, the other two time out of the barrier, step out of lockstep
->    (a stale flag bit lets the next wait through), and PKGB2 parks CPU1 while
->    PKGB1 is still in group 5 -- whose cache-maintenance cross-call to cores
->    0-2 (`sub_40170940(7)` -> `sub_401770BC`) then waits for ever. Traced
->    barrier by barrier over the gdbstub. **With `-machine
->    snor-user=<extracted body>` the loop is 0.01 s, every barrier passes in
->    step, and the crank turns again**: the next stop is an unmodelled read of
->    0x15540018 from pc 0x4026b37c (`sub_4026B364`, a 1 MiB-window accessor
->    with 88 callers). So `snor-user` is no longer optional for this work.
->
-> Instruments and hosts, measured:
->
-> * **Run on exapfs (M4, bare metal).** The development machine is a VM
->   (`Apple M2 Ultra (Virtual)`) and its guest clock advances ~105 RTOS ticks
->   per wall second; on exapfs it is ~665. A firmware that times out at 2000
->   ticks behaves differently on the two.
-> * **`-icount shift=0,sleep=off`** ties guest time to instructions, so a
->   timeout means the same work everywhere and idle periods cost nothing.
->   It did not cure the desync above -- the 2^32 loop is real work -- which is
->   how it was told apart from a host-speed artefact.
-> * **HVF is not worth it yet.** EL3/EL2 appear only in `start`
->   (0x40160000-0x40160258); the runtime has no SMC/HVC and sets only
->   VBAR_EL1, so EL3 alone is not the problem. What is: the RTOS runs Secure
->   EL1 beside Non-secure Linux in one GICv3 with security extensions, and HVF
->   has no secure state; QEMU's hvf asserts on a device access without ISV
->   (`target/arm/hvf/hvf.c`, the data-abort path), so any LDP/STP to MMIO
->   kills the process; unknown sysregs such as CPUECTLR inject UNDEF; RAM
->   must be 16 KiB-aligned; and `-d exec`, `-d int` and `where_blocked.py`
->   -- the method -- are TCG-only. Days of work with lasting divergence.
->
-> **Then the graphics engine.** With SNOR_USER the crank reached
-> 0x15540000, which `../z9-qemu` (WonderLantern, the Z 9 on the same SoC)
-> had already identified as the 2D graphics engine -- same base, same
-> register file, same 0xDECAFACE trailer, even the same microcode
-> selectors. Its readings were re-checked against this image, not copied:
->
-> * **NoC bus windows** (`nikon-z63-noc.h`). The RTOS only reads back the
->   aperture of each DMA window, which the loader sets on the body; here
->   they were zero, so every bus address the firmware derived was garbage.
->   Provisioned at reset with z9's explicit hypothesis, a declared choice.
-> * **XDMAC translates bus addresses** through its group (PERI 1, EXS 2,
->   registered by `sub_40CAA5FC`) and does the DMA memset
->   (`sub_40CAB55C`'s fixed 8-byte source). Until the windows existed, DRAM
->   buffers only worked because the failed lookup left CPU addresses in
->   place.
-> * **The other DSP memories**: measured copies to 0x1c200000, 0x1d200000
->   and 0x1f200000 put each at +0x200000 in its 16 MiB slot.
-> * **The engine itself** (`nikon-z63-gfx.h`): both FIFOs, fences, the DRAM
->   command ring, and the microcode instruction layer (select / parameters
->   / run). Configuration is executed; anything that would draw stops.
->
-> It now stops, on purpose, at the first draw: program 0x281, an image.
-> The firmware has set up the engine, loaded its tables, and is drawing
-> the menu. Rendering is the next piece -- z9 has a functional image, path,
-> glyph and clear renderer for the same microcode, to be ported and
-> re-verified one operation at a time.
->
-> Still open from this round: the 0x17840000 window (EXS1784), whose handler
-> `sub_40C5EA9C` the NAND driver's neighbour installs for SPI 91
-> (`EXS_NETSEC_A`), is still plain storage and unexamined.
-
-Repeat until the GUI task runs: locate the block, identify what it waits for,
-model or wire that, verify progress with `where_blocked.py`. Each turn should
-move the "last application execution" marker measurably later.
-
-What is already known to be waiting and will surface here: the HDMI DDC path
-(costs 1000 ms per transfer and fails until its line exists), the USB assist
-arm, and the card HAL. The region table and the IRQ name table name every
-block that appears; `z63-tools/region_table.py` and `block_identify.py` do the
-naming.
-
-Exit: the UI/menu task is scheduled and running. **Reached.** The module
-start round runs, 189 named tasks exist including the display and UI
-services, and they are blocked on their request queues rather than on
-anything missing from the address map. "What the machine looks like once
-nothing stops it" in `register-windows.md` has the measurements, and the
-phases below now begin from there.
-
-### Where the crank has got to
-
-The turn is now mechanical and is written up in `register-windows.md`: the
-stop names a PC, the PC names a function, the function says in which of three
-shapes its driver gets the device base, and `z63-tools/make_window.py` turns
-that into a modelled block. Seven blocks have gone through it, each one
-surfacing the next:
-
-```
-0x15080044  sensor interface        -> 0x150c0018  ISP_APB device 0x1a0
-0x150c0018  ISP_APB device 0x1a0    -> 0x0e70073c  serial bus, instance 7
-0x0e70073c  serial bus              -> 0x0c86f014  AUX, instance 1
-0x0c86f014  AUX                     -> 0x15200000  the 0xa02e7b0c0 block
-0x15200000  that block              -> 0x15340014  devices 0x185 and 0x186
-0x15340014  devices 0x185/0x186     -> 0x15440308  (next)
-```
-
-Two of those replaced hand-built models that had been built from the accesses
-that happened to have been observed rather than from the driver: the serial
-bus knew three offsets of thirty-six, AUX three of a hundred and twenty-three.
-Each was stopping the machine on the first register its driver reached that
-nobody had yet watched it reach. That is the pattern to expect from any
-remaining block modelled the old way.
-
-Two things found on the way are worth more than the blocks themselves:
-
-* The firmware ships its own **device id to MMIO base table** in `.data`
-  around flat 0x451fe400. A stopping address is usually in it, under an id,
-  with its siblings beside it and its driver's entry points above it.
-* There are two EXPEED 7 IDA databases and only one of them is this image.
-  The other agrees closely enough to be convincing and produces wrong
-  answers. `register-windows.md` has the md5s and the test.
-
-## Phase 3 — pixels
-
-**The device side is done.** `nikon-z63-scanout.h` reads the controller's
-register file and composes a frame from guest DRAM, validated against the
-Z 8 reversing work by `z63-tools/scanout_check.sh`. What is left on this
-phase is not a model: it is the firmware writing the output size. The
-scanout names that register in its refusal message every run. See "Phase 3:
-the display is modelled, with scanout" in `register-windows.md`.
-
-
-The display controller at 0x15480000 / 0x15484000 is modelled as a register
-file. Scanout is not. This phase makes the DISP unit's framebuffer appear on a
-QEMU console: find where the firmware points it, what format it writes, and
-drive a `DisplaySurface` from guest memory. The MIPI DSI host at 0x1548D000 is
-a Synopsys DesignWare v1.3x map and already understood register for register.
-
-Reference, not source: `../n-research/z8-qemu` renders the Z 8's native menu
-through a modelled GPU and LCD controller, and `../z7-qemu` has a Bayer → ISP
-→ LCD pipeline. Neither body's addresses transfer, but both show the shape.
-
-Exit: the menu is on screen.
-
-## Phase 4 — input
-
-Keys, so the menu can be navigated. The Z 8 board drives them through its
-ExMCU over SSP/GPIO; this body's path has not been traced yet.
-
-Exit: the menu responds.
-
-## Phase 5 — CFexpress, by not writing a storage controller
-
-The slot is PCIe + NVMe and the firmware carries EDK2's NVMe driver verbatim
-(`NvmExpress.c`, `NvmExpressDriverBindingStart`, `== NVME IDENTIFY CONTROLLER
-DATA ==`, `Aqa.Asqs`). The root complex is a Synopsys DesignWare — proven by
-`DBI_RO_WR_EN` at DBI+0x8BC written 1 then 0 around the read-only config
-writes, `GEN3_RELATED_OFF` 0x890, `GEN3_EQ_CONTROL_OFF` 0x8A8, the MSI block
-at 0x820-0x830, and the unrolled iATU at DBI+0x300000. Four root ports at
-0x1A000000 / 0x1A400000 / 0x1A800000 / 0x1AC00000, controller wrappers at
-0x17B00000 and 0x17B80000.
-
-So QEMU's own `hw/pci-host/designware.c` plus `-device nvme` can serve this,
-with four gaps to close:
-
-1. QEMU implements only the viewport-indexed iATU at 0x900; this firmware uses
-   the unrolled window at DBI+0x300000 and never touches 0x900.
-2. `designware_pcie_update_viewport()` handles only `ATU_TYPE_MEM`; region 0
-   here is `CFG0`, and that region *is* the config path.
-3. 0x8BC, 0x890, 0x8A8, 0xB40 are unimplemented.
-4. Link state lives outside the DBI, in the wrappers, polled as LTSSM == 0x11.
-   Letting it time out is a legitimate first step: `sub_40289B30` then gates
-   every access and the stack reports "no device" cleanly.
-
-Exit: the firmware enumerates an NVMe namespace.
-
-## Phase 6 — ISP and capture
-
-Furthest out, and not planned in detail until Phase 3 has made the display
-path real. `PERSUIT_0..31`, `RAW0/1`, `IPU`, `RSZ`, `MPO`, `YUV_COMP/DECOMP`,
-`JPEG_0/1`, `HEVC_STL`, `PRORES`, `MVD`, `VMAP` are all named in the IRQ table
-and all live in `ISP_APB` / `ISP_AHB_AXI` / `CODEC` per the region table.
+The original plan (phases 0-6 below) has been worked through: the camera
+boots, draws its menus, takes key presses, runs live view and writes a
+photograph to a CFexpress card. What follows is where it stands, measured,
+what is known not to work, and the order proposed for what is next. The
+history of how it got here is kept short at the end; the commit messages
+carry the detail.
+
+## Where it stands (2026-10-13, `mcu-link` at 3ac1f42)
+
+* **Boot.** The RTOS runs on three A53 cores and starts CPU3 itself
+  (`Z63: released CPU3 at pc=0x40160000`); Linux 4.14 boots there to the
+  BusyBox shell and starts LaMainApp. The body MCU runs its own firmware
+  (`-machine mcu-image=`), with the frame link to the EXPEED, its I2C
+  expanders (the buttons), the camera clock (`mcu-rtc=`), the battery's
+  ready line and the top LCD (`toplcd-live=`).
+* **Display and menus.** The 2D graphics engine renders the GUI's image,
+  path, glyph and clear programs (`nikon-z63-gfx.h`, from `../z9-qemu`'s
+  renderer, re-checked here); the scanout composes the frame
+  (`scanout-dump=`, `scanout-live=`), which z63ui shows. All seven menu
+  tabs walk top to bottom without a stop.
+* **Input.** Keys through the MCU (`mcu-keys=`, or `qom-set mcu-keys` at
+  run time); the shutter button's contacts reach the EXPEED as EXT_INT_4/5.
+* **Live view.** The sensor streams from a Bayer file (`z9-capture-raw=`)
+  through `../z9-qemu`'s capture models (`-machine z9-capture=on`, default
+  props in `nikon-z63-z9lab.h`), the vector DSPs and the display's direct
+  input; frame by frame, with DISP cycling through its screens, the
+  histogram among them (its bins read 0).
+* **Stills.** With custom setting d6 = electronic shutter, a release writes
+  DCIM/xxxNZ6_3/DSC_nnnn.JPG (6048x4032, Exif "NIKON Z6_3") to the card and
+  live view resumes, release after release.
+* **Storage.** NAND (the "ANAND" store, `nand=`), the settings journal in
+  SNOR_USER (written; not saved to the file), a CFexpress card on PCIe
+  (`cfexpress=on`, QEMU's nvme; the camera formats it), an empty SD slot.
+* **Standby.** 30 s after live view starts with no key pressed, the
+  camera's suspend-to-RAM runs to its end -- cores off, DRAM in
+  self-refresh, the power-hold line dropped -- and `-machine power-off=`
+  decides what follows (default: a clean guest shutdown).
+* **Playback.** The JPEG cores decode (`z63-jpeg-decode`): PLAY decodes the
+  newest picture's 1620x1080 preview. It stops at the next step, the
+  resizer (below).
+* **Regression.** `z63-tools/smoke.sh` passes, but checks less than it
+  should: see "Next", item 1.
+
+## Known not to work
+
+Ranked by how much of the camera each one blocks.
+
+1. **Playback stops at the resizer.** After the decode, the resizer scales
+   the picture from RAM: A1 (0x15421100) `+4 = 0x5001` at pc 0x40c27448,
+   with the coefficient banks 0x15427000/0x15428000 and output instance
+   0x15432000. `../z9-qemu` models RAM input only for the thumbnail route
+   (A2 -> F4 -> B4, `nikon-z9-thumbnail-nv16.h`).
+2. **Shutter types other than electronic stall.** At auto, mechanical and
+   EFCS the still waits for image outputs that never come (items 0x274F/
+   0x2750 = 0x2A01/0x2038, live-view readout type 2).
+3. **A d6 change after an early half-press is late.** Half-press, then set
+   d6 to electronic: a release within ~10 s of leaving the menu still takes
+   the old shutter's path and stops on the JPEG core (0x15e20400). After
+   ~70 s idle it works. Fourteen settings items differ between the two
+   states (0x1526, 0x20a2, 0x20d6, 0x20e6, 0x242f, 0x24ab/ac, 0x270a/b,
+   0x4001, 0x4800/3, 0x5701, 0x58b); what decides is not known.
+4. **No wake from standby, and no standby after a key.** An untouched boot
+   goes to standby at ~68 s and, with the default, the machine shuts down.
+   After any key press the camera never goes to standby at all, which the
+   body would after 30 s -- unexplained. The wake is understood (below) but
+   not modelled.
+5. **Settings do not persist across boots.** SNOR_USER writes are not
+   saved (an opt-in `snor-user-save=` was proposed), so d6 must be set
+   every boot.
+6. **Smaller ones, each seen once:** live-view zoom ends in camera error
+   0x40 with the scanout stopped; AF-ON with no lens leaves the camera
+   unresponsive; Setup > clean image sensor NACKs on I2C bank 2 address
+   0x34; voice-memo options and the colour-balance picture use GFX draws
+   the model skips (YUV targets, sampler mode); movie recording, the VPU
+   (0x16010000) and ProRes (0x16000000) have not been reached.
+
+Deliberately not modelled: the ARM IPCU at 0x0C851000 (a storage stand-in
+would make sends "succeed" and then hang -- never reached), the NOR
+calibration commands of sub_455CB8CC (never reached).
+
+## Next, in the order proposed
+
+1. **Make smoke check Linux again.** `z63-tools/frontier` still records
+   0x15540000, which no longer stops anything, and its note says Linux is
+   broken in the full machine -- it is not any more (BusyBox, LaMainApp).
+   Clear the line so smoke's Linux check runs, with `power-off=continue`
+   so the 68 s standby does not end the run. Cheap, and it guards the
+   whole machine instead of half of it.
+2. **Playback's resize.** Model the RAM-input route the playback job
+   programs (item 1 above): A1's input plane and geometry, the scaler
+   instance and the 0x1543 output, and the completions its job waits for.
+   PLAY should then show the picture.
+3. **Wake from standby.** Static analysis found it a warm reboot, not a jump
+   back: the MCU's power manager polls the power-hold line (MCU PG12 <-
+   EXPEED port 3 bit 11, inferred) after the power-off frame, cuts every
+   EXPEED rail but DRAM's (PE11), and on an enabled wake line restores
+   them, raises the resume flag (PD10 -> port 3 bit 6) and releases the
+   EXPEED's reset (PC4). The EXPEED's start-up takes port 3 bit 6 high as a
+   resume (the DRAM init's mode 3; sub_40D6F7F0 at 0x40d6f844 with byte
+   0xa1bff7880). Needed: the two wires, an EXPEED-only reset that keeps
+   DRAM and the MCU, the CPU3 hand-off re-armed. Together with finding why
+   a key press disables standby, this is the "keep it awake" the z63ui
+   user asked for.
+4. **The other shutter types** (item 2), then **the late d6** (item 3):
+   both are in the capture planning, and the electronic-shutter path that
+   works is the reference.
+5. **Persist settings** behind an opt-in property (item 5).
+6. The histogram's bins (computed from the RAW pipeline's frame), and the
+   smaller items as the UI sweep reaches them.
+
+## How the work is done
+
+The method has not changed and is written up in `register-windows.md`: a
+stop names a PC, the PC names a function, the function says how its driver
+gets the device base, and the block is modelled from the driver, not from
+the accesses that happened to be seen. The instruments, all one command:
+
+* `z63-tools/where_blocked.py`, `tasks.py` (every RTOS task by name and
+  state), `gdb_probe.py`, and the TCG plugins `z63log` (camera errors,
+  function calls and callers), `z63watch`, `z63ftrace`;
+* the idasql databases of the EXPEED image, the loader and the MCU
+  firmware, and `make_window.py` / `handle_offsets.py` /
+  `window_pointers.py` for register sets;
+* sweeps: a static one of every fixed device address the image
+  materialises against the board's windows, and a scripted UI walk that
+  attributes each stop to the key that caused it (`shots.sh` steps).
+
+`../z9-qemu`'s capture models come in through `z63-tools/import_z9lab.py`,
+which writes `hw/arm/nikon-z63-z9lab.c` and reproduces it exactly: change
+the script (each Z 6III edit sits behind a `z63-*` switch documented in
+`z63-tools/z9lab_attach.c.in`), never the generated file.
+
+Run on exapfs (M4, bare metal): the development VM's guest clock runs ~6x
+slower, and firmware timeouts behave differently on the two.
+
+## How it got here
+
+* **Phase 0-1, integration and interrupts.** HDMI and USB wired; the board
+  learned to raise interrupts only when the firmware arms them; the IRQ
+  name table (rodata 0x41847000, SPI *n* at +0x20n) names every line.
+* **Phase 2, the crank.** The loader band and the image placed in DRAM
+  where the firmware's page tables expect it (undefined instructions
+  102,715 per 30 s -> 0); the NAND controller (EXS1780); the per-unit
+  SNOR_USER body (without it a 2^32 loop desynchronises the module
+  lifecycle); dozens of blocks modelled from their drivers, the history in
+  `z63-tools/frontier`. Exit reached: 189 named tasks, the GUI among them.
+* **Phase 3, pixels.** The scanout composes frames; the NoC bus windows,
+  XDMAC's bus translation and the DSP memories made the GUI's buffers real;
+  the graphics engine's draw programs were ported and re-verified one at a
+  time.
+* **Phase 4, input.** The body MCU's own firmware, run as a second machine
+  (an STM32 model), linked to the EXPEED by its SPI frames and handshake
+  wires; buttons through its I2C expanders; MENU opens.
+* **Phase 5, CFexpress.** QEMU's DesignWare host with the unrolled iATU,
+  the CFG0 region, the link wrappers, and `-device nvme`.
+* **Phase 6, ISP and capture.** `../z9-qemu`'s capture models (revision
+  deb3a75) in front of the board's windows; the DSPs' bus windows widened
+  to all of DRAM (the live-view stall); the shutter mechanism answered by
+  the MCU; the still through engine 0x150c, its frame end and the JPEG
+  cores (`z63-still-capture`).
+* **This round.** Standby to its end (`power-off=`); a static sweep closed
+  the register gaps it found (sensor interface banks, devices 0x184 and
+  0x1cc-0x1ce, EXS1784); the histogram; JPEG decode.
 
